<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
    <meta charset="UTF-8">
    <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0">
    <title>Session Fixation</title>
    <style>
        body {
            background-color: #111;
            color: #f3f3f3;
            font-family: Arial, sans-serif;
            margin: 2rem;
        }

        .wrap {
            max-width: 900px;
            margin: 0 auto;
        }

        .card {
            border: 1px solid #333;
            background: #1a1a1a;
            padding: 1.5rem;
            margin-bottom: 1.5rem;
            border-radius: 8px;
        }

        h1, h2, h3 {
            margin-top: 0;
        }

        form {
            margin-bottom: 1rem;
        }

        label {
            display: block;
            margin-bottom: 0.25rem;
            font-weight: bold;
        }

        input {
            width: 100%;
            max-width: 420px;
            box-sizing: border-box;
            padding: 0.55rem;
            margin-bottom: 0.75rem;
            border-radius: 4px;
            border: 1px solid #555;
            background: #000;
            color: #fff;
        }

        button {
            background: #4CAF50;
            color: white;
            border: none;
            padding: 0.75rem 1rem;
            border-radius: 4px;
            cursor: pointer;
            margin-right: 0.75rem;
        }

        .status {
            background: #0f1f12;
            border: 1px solid #25432a;
            padding: 1rem;
            border-radius: 6px;
        }

        code {
            background: #000;
            border: 1px solid #333;
            padding: 0.2rem 0.4rem;
            border-radius: 4px;
            word-break: break-all;
        }

        ul {
            padding-left: 1.2rem;
        }
    </style>
</head>
<body>
    <div class="wrap">
        <div class="card">
            <h1>Session Fixation</h1>
            <p>
                Session fixation happens when an attacker can force a victim to use a known session identifier,
                then authenticates the victim without rotating that identifier. The attacker can reuse the same
                session ID after the victim logs in.
            </p>
            <p>
                This training challenge keeps the exercise local and synthetic. It never uses real accounts or external
                services. The demo credentials are intentionally hard-coded for the lab only.
            </p>
        </div>

        <div class="card">
            <h2>Current session state</h2>
            <div class="status">
                <p><strong>Initialized:</strong> <%= state.initialized ? 'Yes' : 'No' %></p>
                <p><strong>Authenticated:</strong> <%= state.authenticated ? 'Yes' : 'No' %></p>
                <p><strong>Current synthetic user:</strong> <%= state.username || 'None' %></p>
                <p><strong>Auth method:</strong> <%= state.authMethod || 'None' %></p>
                <p><strong>Session ID:</strong> <code><%= state.sessionId %></code></p>
            </div>

            <% if (message) { %>
                <p><em><%= message %></em></p>
            <% } %>
        </div>

        <div class="card">
            <h2>1. Initialize a pre-authentication session</h2>
            <form action="/dashboard/level1/session-fixation/init" method="post">
                <button type="submit">Initialize pre-authentication session</button>
            </form>
        </div>

        <div class="card">
            <h2>2. Demonstration: vulnerable login</h2>
            <p>
                This route intentionally keeps the existing session identifier after authentication. The same session remains
                attached to the attacker-controlled ID.
            </p>
            <form action="/dashboard/level1/session-fixation/vulnerable-login" method="post">
                <label for="vulnerable-username">Synthetic username</label>
                <input id="vulnerable-username" name="username" type="text" value="session_fixation_demo" required>

                <label for="vulnerable-password">Synthetic password</label>
                <input id="vulnerable-password" name="password" type="password" value="session_fixation_pass" required>

                <button type="submit">Run vulnerable login</button>
            </form>
        </div>

        <div class="card">
            <h2>3. Demonstration: secure login</h2>
            <p>
                This route validates the same synthetic credentials but regenerates the session identifier before setting
                the authenticated state. The old session can no longer access the authenticated session.
            </p>
            <form action="/dashboard/level1/session-fixation/secure-login" method="post">
                <label for="secure-username">Synthetic username</label>
                <input id="secure-username" name="username" type="text" value="session_fixation_demo" required>

                <label for="secure-password">Synthetic password</label>
                <input id="secure-password" name="password" type="password" value="session_fixation_pass" required>

                <button type="submit">Run secure login</button>
            </form>
        </div>

        <div class="card">
            <h2>4. Session check</h2>
            <p>Use this endpoint to inspect the current authenticated state in a browser or by API request.</p>
            <a href="/dashboard/level1/session-fixation/status"><button type="button">View current session state</button></a>
        </div>

        <div class="card">
            <h2>5. Reset the demonstration</h2>
            <form action="/dashboard/level1/session-fixation/reset" method="post">
                <button type="submit">Reset session</button>
            </form>
        </div>

        <div class="card">
            <h2>Visible comparison</h2>
            <ul>
                <li><strong>Vulnerable flow:</strong> keeps the same session ID after login.</li>
                <li><strong>Secure flow:</strong> regenerates the session ID before auth state is assigned.</li>
                <li><strong>Remediation:</strong> rotate the session ID after authentication and discard the pre-authentication session.</li>
            </ul>
        </div>
    </div>
</body>
</html>
